"""The Defect's strategy, shared by planning and individual decisions.

Owner's guidance: mixed playstyles and a few archetypes, generally with Frost Orbs; look
for scaling with either Orbs or physical attacks.

The numbers in the text are what the highest-rated Spire Codex players did with the Defect on the
pinned game version: 133 solo A10 standard runs of builds v0.107.1 and v0.107.0 by the 12 highest-rated
accounts that have them, 96 won, read 2026-11-25 with ``python -m sts_agent.pilot players --character
DEFECT --words Lightning,Focus`` (the decks carried into each act boss and what was taken at 2419 card
rewards, counted by ``evidence.deck_balance``'s role rules). The build names come from the Spire Codex
guides (builds guide and Defect tier list), which describe later patches.
"""

PROMPT_VERSION = "defect-strategic-planner-prompt-3"

DECK_GUIDANCE = """\
THE DEFECT'S DECK. The Defect wins with several builds and usually with a mix of them, so judge the deck by what it does this fight, not by an archetype. What the mix looks like is known. In 133 solo A10 runs on this game version by the highest-rated Spire Codex players (96 won), the deck entering the act 1, 2 and 3 bosses held about 19, 27 and 35 cards, with as many Block cards as damage cards (four, seven and nine of each), 1.4, 2.3 and 2.9 cards that Channel Frost, and one Focus source from act 2. It is not kept thin: they took 89% of the card rewards in act 1, 73% in act 2 and 62% in act 3, as often with a large deck as with a small one.

Defense is Frost in nearly every build: a Channeled Frost Orb gains Block at the end of every turn for as long as it stays in a slot, and again when it is Evoked. Those players took a Frost card half the time it was offered until the deck held two, about a third of the time with two or three, and rarely a fifth. The deck_balance track "frost" asks for two, three in a deck over 24 cards, and marks Frost over-supplied from four; while it is missing or short a Frost card is the first defensive pick, ahead of a plain Block card of similar size. A Block card was taken nearly half the time as one of the first three, a third of the time up to the sixth and a quarter after, so Block is never marked over-supplied. One card that applies Weak or lowers enemy Strength was taken two times in three while the deck had none and seldom after (the track "weak" asks for one). Orb slots are shared: Channeling into full slots Evokes the leftmost Orb, so count the Orbs the deck Channels in a turn against its slots before adding more generators, and do not Evoke away the Frost the defense stands on. The starter's Lightning deals the early damage; it is not a defense, and cards that only Channel Lightning were taken one time in ten.

Scaling comes from Orbs or from attacks, and the deck already leans one way; build that one and take the other only when it costs nothing. Orbs: Focus raises what every Orb does, Frost's Block included; Focus, more slots and Powers that add Block or Orbs every turn were taken four times in ten, deck_balance counts them as scaling for both damage and Block, and a second is worth taking while the deck holds one. This route needs Orbs that stay in play, so it wants Frost and slots before Focus. Attacks: an attack that raises its own damage every time it is played, a zero-cost Attack engine with the cards that return or replay them (the build the community guides rate first), or lasting Strength; this route wants cheap attacks, draw and removals, and its Orb slots are spare. What those players took less and less is damage cards: one in three for the first two, one in five up to the fifth and one in eight after; deck_balance marks damage over-supplied once the deck holds more damage cards than those decks did (from the sixth in act 1, the eighth in act 2, the tenth after). Draw, energy and other utility were taken three times in ten in every act whatever the deck already held: keep adding them. A Power that needs several turns to pay back is weighed against the act boss's first big attack: it is in play early or it is a skip. Removals: about three a run, most of them bought at act 2 shops, starter Strikes first. Status cards a card adds to the deck are a cost every later draw pays unless something held turns them into value."""

ELITE_GUIDANCE = """\
THE DEFECT'S ELITE ROUTES. Follow the act stance below. Elites cost the Defect about what they cost the Ironclad in both acts (in simulated A10 runs a strong drafter lost about one in thirteen and gave up a quarter of max HP for one it beat), so route toward them in act 1 and keep taking them later. The highest-rated players do: 2.4 elites in act 1, 2.1 in act 2 and 1.9 in act 3 a run, losing 17%, 19% and 13% of max HP to each, with a deck that by the act 1 boss holds about ten added cards: four that deal damage, four Block cards of which one or two Channel Frost, one scaling piece and two draw or utility cards. What differs from the Ironclad is recovery: nothing heals the Defect after a fight, so place a rest site before or right behind an elite when the map allows, and have Frost or non-starter Block in the deck before an elite with one big attack turn. Say in the plan which elites are accepted and what the deck must pick up before each."""
